    id: "local-global-attention",
    term: "local+global attention",
    category: "general",
    aka: ["local global attention", "sparse attention", "sliding window attention", "gated attention"],
    summary: "An attention pattern that combines a local sliding-window mechanism — attending to nearby tokens — with a global mechanism — attending to a sparse set of tokens across the entire sequence — to achieve near-quadratic-attention quality at substantially reduced compute and memory cost for long sequences.",
    explanation: "Local-global attention refers to a family of attention patterns that decompose the O(n^2) full-attention computation into a local component that handles short-range token interactions and a global component that handles long-range ones. The motivation is the standard Transformer's fundamental scaling limitation: attention between all pairs of tokens costs O(n^2) in sequence length, which becomes intractable for sequences of tens of thousands of tokens (the practical context-length limit for unrestricted GPT-3 era models).\n\nThe local component is typically a sliding window — at each token, attention is computed only against the w nearest tokens (where w is small, e.g., 256 or 1024). This gives O(n * w) local attention computation. The global component uses a smaller set of 'global' tokens (sometimes called sink, landmark, or anchor tokens) that all positions attend to and that attend to all positions — these propagate information across the sequence at reduced cost.\n\nSpecific implementations vary. Longformer (Beltagy et al., 2020) uses windowed attention for most tokens plus distinguished global-attention positions specified by the user. Big Bird (Zaheer et al., 2020) adds random attention blocks for theoretical reasons related to universal approximation. Longnet (Ding et al., 2023) dilates the attention window with exponentially growing stride, allowing linear-cost attention at very long ranges. Modern long-context LLMs (Llama 3, Qwen 2, Mistral) frequently use sliding-window attention combined with grouped-query attention and rotary positional encodings to support 32k-128k or larger context lengths.\n\nThe pattern is similar in spirit to classical signal processing — local filters capture fine structure, while global operations capture long-range dependencies. In the attention context, the local part handles syntactic and short-range semantic phenomena; the global part handles topic-level coherence and retrieval of information across far-apart parts of the input. The flexibility to vary the local window size and global-token selection allows architectures to trade compute for quality in different ways.",
    howItWorks: "Local sliding window attention. For each position i, the attention keys and values used are those at positions in [i - w, i + w], where w is the window size. The attention matrix is block-tridiagonal, reducing computation from O(n^2) to O(n * w). Dilation extends the effective receptive field: with dilation d, the window samples positions [i - d*w, i - (d-1)*d, ..., i, ..., i + (d-1)*d, i + d*w], covering a range of 2dw with the same w queries per token. Stacked dilated layers (each layer dilates more aggressively) build a logarithmically growing effective receptive field with depth.\n\nGlobal tokens. Designate a small set of G token positions (or virtual tokens, like 'sink tokens' introduced by StreamingLLM) to receive global attention — they attend to all positions and are attended to by all positions. Information flows: token i attends locally to its neighbors; the neighbor attends to a global token; the global token attends to a far-away token; the reverse path lets information from far-away positions propagate back to i. Total cost: O(n * w + n * G).\n\nLongformer combines the two: windowed attention for all positions plus user-specified global-attention positions for tokens that should see and be seen by everything (e.g., classification token, section headers). Big Bird adds random attention — each local window plus a few random other windows — which is theoretically motivated by universal approximation properties.\n\nStreamingLLM and similar efficient long-context approaches introduce sink tokens at the sequence start that act as attention sinks — they absorb off-distribution attention mass that would otherwise destabilize attention at long sequence lengths, allowing the model to handle arbitrarily long sequences during inference with fixed memory by attending to a sliding window of recent tokens plus the sinks.",
    whyItMatters: "Long-context capability is one of the most consequential capabilities of modern LLMs — it determines whether models can read entire documents, process codebases, ingest retrieval-augmented context, or analyze hours-long audio. Without efficient long-sequence attention, context windows would be stuck at the few-thousand-token limit of the original Transformer, severely limiting application scope.\n\nLocal-global patterns enable context lengths that would be intractable with full attention. Modern frontier models supporting 128k-1M token contexts rely on some combination of sliding-window attention, sparse global tokens, grouped-query attention (reducing KV-cache memory), and ring attention (distributing the attention computation across multiple devices). The engineering of these patterns is a competitive moat for frontier labs.\n\nBeyond efficiency, the local-global structure also influences model behavior. Long-context retrieval tasks (Needle-in-a-Haystack) require the global component to surface information from far-away positions; models with weak global attention (e.g., naive sliding-window-only variants) fail at long-range retrieval even if their average loss is low. The design of attention patterns has thus become a question of both efficiency and capability, not merely a computational trade-off.",
    history: "The local-global attention lineage begins with Longformer (Beltagy et al., 2020), which combined window attention with user-specified global-attention positions specifically for long-document tasks. Big Bird (Zahe et al., 2020) provided a theoretical justification of sparse attention via universal approximation properties and added random attention to its local+global mix.\n\nIn parallel, techniques like dilated convolutions and dilated attention (Longnet, Ding et al., 2023) extended the effective receptive field logarithmically with depth. Sparse Transformers (Child et al., 2019) introduced earlier factored attention patterns for image and audio generation.\n\nThe modern LLM era integrated these patterns into production architectures. LLaMA-2 and Mistral use sliding-window attention combined with rotary positional encoding. Long-context extension techniques like YaRN (Peng et al., 2023) and LongRoPE extend the effective context length further by interpolating rotary positional encodings. StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023) introduced sink tokens that stabilize attention at very long context lengths, enabling infinite-context inference. Ring attention (Liu et al., 2023) and Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) addressed the compute and memory cost of attention at scale through system-level innovations rather than architectural sparsity, and recent frontier models combine all of these techniques to support context windows in the million-token range.",
    relatedConcepts: ["attention-mechanism", "transformer", "long-context", "sparse-attention", "flash-attention"],
    relatedPapers: [],
    discoverers: "Iz Beltagy, Manzil Zaheer, Sameer Singh",
    yearDiscovered: 2020,
    tags: ["attention", "long-context", "sparse", "transformer"]
